cid]\\n        clusters_out.append({'cluster_id': cid, 'size': c['size'], 'stability': c['stability']})\\n    selected_out = sorted(list(selected))\\n    return {'clusters': clusters_out, 'selected_cluster_ids': selected_out}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that implements condensed tree stability selection. Given the payload: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of dicts each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null). Task: drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size. For each retained cluster, compute stability: sum over points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). For each point, exit_lambda from point_exit_lambdas list? But each point has its own lambda? The birth_lambda is per cluster (same for all points). The point_exit_lambdas is a list of exit lambdas for each point in the cluster. So stability = sum_{point} max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Then rank clusters by decreasing stability, then by (presumably) cluster_id. Then select clusters: only select a cluster if no already selected descendant blocks it (descendant meaning child/grandchild etc. in parent tree). After selecting a cluster, block its ancestors from being selected (since ancestors are not selected if descendant selected). Actually typical HDBSCAN condensed tree selection: select clusters with highest stability, but if a cluster is selected, its ancestors are not selected. And a cluster can only be selected if no child (descendant) is already selected. So we need to process in order of decreasing stability (and tie-break by cluster_id). For each cluster, check if any of its descendants (in the tree) have already been selected. If none, then select it, and mark all its ancestors as ineligible (or we can just skip ancestors when we encounter them later). Since we process in order, we can maintain a set of selected_cluster_ids and a set of blocked_ancestors. But careful: if we select a cluster, we should not later select any ancestor. So when considering a cluster, if it is in blocked_ancestors (i.e., already blocked due to a descendant selected), skip. Also need to check if any of its descendants are selected? Actually if we process in order, the descendant might have been processed earlier if it has higher stability. But descendant could be lower stability, so later we might encounter parent first? Actually we process from highest stability down. So descendant will be processed before parent if it has higher stability, but if descendant has lower stability, parent might be processed first. In that case, parent might be selected initially, but later descendant with lower stability should not be selected because its ancestor is selected? The rule: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That means if a descendant is already selected, the ancestor cannot be selected. So when we process a cluster, we need to check if any of its descendants have been selected. If so, skip. If we select a cluster, then block its ancestors (so they won't be selected later). So we need to know descendants of each cluster. We can build a tree structure from parent mapping. Also need to maintain set of selected. We'll process sorted clusters (by stability descending, then cluster_id ascending for tie). For each cluster in that order, if it is not blocked, check if any descendant in the subtree is selected. If none, select it and block all its ancestors. Return list of clusters (stability rows) and selected_cluster_ids (sorted? The schema says selected_cluster_ids is array of strings, no order specified. But typical HDBSCAN returns sorted? It says \"sorted selected IDs\" in description. So presumably sort them. Also return clusters array containing dicts with cluster_id, size, stability for all retained clusters (even those not selected). The output schema requires clusters array with all retained clusters.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case: validate required fields: min_cluster_size (int), clusters (list), parent (dict). Ensure clusters list length >0 and each cluster has required fields: cluster_id (string), size (int), birth_lambda (number), point_exit_lambdas (list of numbers). Also parent must contain entries for all cluster_ids (including null for root). Raise ValueError if any missing or invalid.\n2. Filter clusters by size >= min_cluster_size. Keep only those clusters.\n3. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum(max(0, exit - birth) for exit in point_exit_lambdas). Note: birth is per cluster, exit per point.\n4. Build tree: create adjacency from parent to children. Also keep a map of cluster_id to cluster data.\n5. Create list of (cluster_id, stability) for retained clusters. Sort by (-stability, cluster_id) (since stability descending, then cluster_id ascending for tie).\n6. Initialize selected set, blocked set (ancestors blocked due to descendant selection).\n7. For tree traversal, we need to compute descendants for each node to check if any descendant is selected. Could precompute all descendants sets for each node, or perform DFS on the fly when considering a cluster. Since number of clusters is small (likely), we can compute descendants recursively. Or we can compute ancestors for each node and mark blocked ancestors upon selection. To check for descendant selection, we can have a map cluster -> list of descendants (including itself? but we need strict descendant? \"no already selected descendant\" - descendant means child, grandchild etc., not itself). So for each cluster, we need to check if any of its descendants (children and their children) are in selected set. We can precompute descendant sets for all nodes (by traversing from leaves up or using recursion). Since tree is directed from parent to child (parent map gives parent from child). We can build children lists.\n8. Process sorted clusters:\n   - If cluster_id is in blocked set, skip.\n   - Check if any descendant of cluster_id is selected. Use precomputed descendant set. If yes, skip.\n   - Else: add cluster_id to selected set, and block all its ancestors. To block ancestors, we need to traverse up the parent chain (using parent map) and add those to blocked set.\n9. After processing, gather all retained clusters into list of dicts with cluster_id, size, stability (as float). Sort selected_cluster_ids (by string order? The description says \"sorted selected IDs\", presumably lexicographically). The output schema doesn't specify order, but we can sort them.\n10. Return {\"clusters\": [...], \"selected_cluster_ids\": [...]}.\n\nEdge cases:\n- If no clusters retained, clusters empty list, selected empty list.\n- Ensure stability is number (float). Could be integer if all differences integer, but output type number is fine.\n- Validate parent mapping: should be consistent (no cycles, each cluster_id appears exactly once as key, value is either null or another cluster_id that exists). But given task, we can assume valid or raise ValueError if inconsistency.\n- The min_cluster_size might be such that some clusters are dropped, but their children might still exist? Actually if a parent is dropped, its children still exist? The condensed tree construction typically removes clusters below min size and then reconnects children to the next ancestor? But here the instruction says: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" But then we need to consider the hierarchy of retained clusters? The parent mapping given might include dropped clusters as parents. When dropping a cluster, its children should be re-parented to the nearest ancestor above min size? The description doesn't specify. However typical HDBSCAN condensed tree selection does re-parenting: after pruning small clusters, children of pruned clusters are attached to the nearest ancestor that is retained. But the instruction says \"For each retained cluster sum...\" It doesn't mention re-parenting. Also \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" - the descendant relationship should be based on the original hierarchy or after pruning? The original hierarchy is given by parent. If we drop a cluster, its descendants are still descendants in the original tree. But if the parent is dropped, the descendant relationship should still hold? In HDBSCAN, the tree is a rooted tree at null parent. The condensed tree is built by removing clusters below min size, and then linking children to the next surviving ancestor. But the instruction only says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" It doesn't say to re-link. The selection algorithm then operates on the surviving clusters. However the descendant blocking: if a cluster is selected, it blocks its ancestors (including dropped ones?). But dropped clusters are not in retained list, so they aren't considered. The blocking should only apply to retained clusters. So we need to consider the hierarchy among retained clusters. If a retained cluster's parent was dropped, then its ancestor is the next retained ancestor up. We need to recompute ancestors/descendants for only retained clusters after dropping small ones.\n\nLet's examine the public fixture: It shows one case with clusters c0(size7), c1(size4), c2(size2), min_cluster_size=3. So c2 (size2) is dropped. Parent mapping: c0 root, c1 parent c0, c2 parent c0. After dropping c2, retained clusters c0 and c1. Their parent relationship: c1 parent is c0 (still). So hierarchy remains same because c2 is not a parent of any retained cluster. But if c2 had a child that's retained, then that child's parent would be c2 which is dropped. So we need to handle re-parenting. The description from the candidate artifact says \"Condense a supplied hierarchy under a minimum cluster size\" which implies typical condensed tree construction: remove clusters below min size and re-link children to the nearest surviving ancestor. Also the phrase \"valid_condensed_tree\" suggests we must produce a valid condensed tree. So we must implement the re-parenting.\n\nThus steps:\n- Drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n- For each remaining cluster, find its nearest ancestor (including itself? Actually we want the parent in the condensed tree to be the first ancestor that is not dropped). If no such ancestor, parent is null (root). Build new parent mapping for retained clusters.\n- Then compute stability for retained clusters (using original birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas, unchanged).\n- The tree for selection should be based on this condensed hierarchy.\n\nImplementation plan for re-parenting:\n- Build a mapping from cluster_id to its data (including parent).\n- Create set of retained_cluster_ids.\n- For each retained cluster, traverse up the original parent chain until we find a retained cluster or null. That becomes its new parent. Store new_parent.\n- Also maintain children list for retained clusters.\n- Need to ensure no cycles (original parent chain should not have cycles, but we can assume valid).\n\nAlternatively, we can build children list from original parent mapping, then perform a DFS on all original clusters, but only keep retained ones, and reattach children of dropped clusters to the nearest retained ancestor.\n\nSimplify: For each cluster in retained set, we can find its condensed parent by walking up the original parent map until we hit a retained node or null. Since the original tree is a tree (no cycles), this will terminate.\n\nThen we have condensed tree with retained clusters. Then compute descendant sets for those retained clusters (using children pointers from condensed parent). Then proceed with stability ranking and selection.\n\nNow, the \"descendants\" in the selection step refer to descendants in the condensed tree (after re-parenting). Since we have re-parented children of dropped clusters to the next ancestor, those children become siblings with the ancestor's other children. The blocking logic: if a cluster is selected, its ancestors (in condensed tree) are blocked. That seems correct.\n\nNow, the output \"clusters\" should contain all retained clusters with cluster_id, size, stability. The \"selected_cluster_ids\" are those selected.\n\nNow tie-breaking: sort by (-stability, cluster_id). Use string comparison for cluster_id.\n\nNow, we need to write solve(case) in Python.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use standard library only.\n- Return dict with keys \"clusters\" and \"selected_cluster_ids\".\n- Raise ValueError on invalid input.\n- Do not mutate case.\n\nWe must encode the Python source as a JSON string inside {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. So we need to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's write a clean function.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate fields\n    required = ['min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent']\n    for k in required:\n        if k not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing field: {k}\")\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_in = case['clusters']\n    parent_in = case['parent']\n    if not isinstance(clusters_in, list) or len(clusters_in) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"clusters must be non-empty list\")\n    if not isinstance(parent_in, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"parent must be dict\")\n    \n    # map cluster_id to data\n    cluster_data = {}\n    for c in clusters_in:\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\n        if not cid:\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster missing cluster_id\")\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster_id must be string\")\n        for field in ['size', 'birth_lambda', 'point_exit_lambdas']:\n            if field not in c:\n                raise ValueError(f\"cluster {cid} missing {field}\")\n        if not isinstance(c['size'], int) or c['size'] < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"size must be non-negative integer\")\n        if not isinstance(c['birth_lambda'], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"birth_lambda must be number\")\n        exits = c['point_exit_lambdas']\n        if not isinstance(exits, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"point_exit_lambdas must be list\")\n        if len(exits) != c['size']:\n            raise ValueError(f\"point_exit_lambdas length {len(exits)} != size {c['size']}\")\n        for e in exits:\n            if not isinstance(e, (int, float)):\n                raise ValueError(\"exit lambda must be number\")\n        cluster_data[cid] = c\n    \n    # validate parent mapping: all cluster IDs present as keys, values either null or existing cluster ID\n    for cid in cluster_data:\n        if cid not in parent_in:\n            raise ValueError(f\"parent mapping missing key {cid}\")\n        p = parent_in[cid]\n        if p is not None and p not in cluster_data:\n            raise ValueError(f\"parent {p} for {cid} not a cluster\")\n        cluster_data[cid]['parent'] = p  # store\n\n    # Drop small clusters\n    retained = {cid: c for cid, c in cluster_data.items() if c['size'] >= min_size}\n    if not retained:\n        return {\"clusters\": [], \"selected_cluster_ids\": []}\n    \n    # Build condensed tree: compute new parent for each retained cluster\n    # First, find condensed parent by walking up original parent chain until retained or None\n    def get_condensed_parent(cid):\n        cur = cid\n        while True:\n            p = cluster_data[cur]['parent']\n            if p is None:\n                return None\n            if p in retained:\n                return p\n            cur = p\n            # infinite loop protection: break if cycle (not expected)\n    \n    for cid in retained:\n        retained[cid]['condensed_parent'] = get_condensed_parent(cid)\n    \n    # Build children mapping for condensed tree\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\n    for cid, c in retained.items():\n        cp = c['condensed_parent']\n        if cp is not None:\n            children[cp].append(cid)\n    \n    # Compute descendant sets for each retained node (including itself? We need descendants excluding self for checking)\n    # Precompute all descendants using DFS or post-order traversal.\n    descendants = {}\n    def compute_descendants(cid):\n        de = set()\n        for ch in children[cid]:\n            de.update(compute_descendants(ch))\n            de.add(ch)\n        descendants[cid] = de\n        return de\n    for cid in retained:\n        if cid not in descendants:\n            compute_descendants(cid)\n    \n    # Compute stability for each retained cluster\n    for cid, c in retained.items():\n        birth = c['birth_lambda']\n        exits = c['point_exit_lambdas']\n        stability = sum(max(0, e - birth) for e in exits)\n        c['stability'] = stability\n    \n    # Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id)\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['stability'], x['cluster_id']))\n    \n    selected = set()\n    blocked_ancestors = set()\n    \n    for c in sorted_clusters:\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\n        if cid in blocked_ancestors:\n            continue\n        # check if any descendant in selected\n        if any(d in selected for d in descendants[cid]):\n            continue\n        # select this cluster\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # block its ancestors: walk up condensed parent chain\n        cur = cid\n        while True:\n            p = retained[cur].get('condensed_parent')\n            if p is None:\n                break\n            blocked_ancestors.add(p)\n            cur = p\n            # safety: if p same as cur? not possible.\n    \n    # Build output for all retained clusters\n    clusters_out = []\n    for cid in sorted(retained.keys()):  # maybe order by cluster_id? Not specified; but alphabetical is okay.\n        c = retained[cid]\n        clusters_out.append({\n            \"cluster_id\": cid,\n            \"size\": c['size'],\n            \"stability\": c['stability']\n        })\n    # Alternatively sort by stability? The schema doesn't require order. We'll sort by cluster_id for determinism.\n    clusters_out.sort(key=lambda x: x['cluster_id'])\n    \n    selected_out = sorted(list(selected))\n    \n    return {\"clusters\": clusters_out, \"selected_cluster_ids\": selected_out}\n\nWe must ensure stability is number (float). Use .0 if integer? Type number includes ints, fine.\n\nNow, write the implementation as a JSON string.\n\nWe need to escape: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". and also escape any other special characters.\n\nWe'll write the Python code and then convert to JSON string manually or using a script. Since I'm an AI, I can produce the escaped string.\n\nLet's produce the final answer with {\"implementation\": \"<escaped Python code>\"}\n\nMake sure no extra text."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1724,"completion_tokens":5021,"total_tokens":6745,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3944},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":316},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}